  <HOMEWORK title="DDR Bring-Up 1: Does the chip even answer?" prompt="First power-on of a new DDR byte lane: CK is toggling, the controller is issuing commands, but the built-in memory test reports nothing but timeouts - every single read comes back unanswered. Before training anything, confirm the most basic thing: is this chip listening on the chip-select line the controller is actually asserting?"
            hint="Compare the controller's &quot;Target chip&quot; setting against this chip's own &quot;Chip number&quot; strap. Do they actually agree?"
            explanation="The controller is addressing chip 0, but this chip is strapped as chip 1 - so it never asserts its own CS line while the controller's commands are flying, and every read times out waiting for an answer that was never going to come. This is the first thing a real bring-up checks, before any training: does the part even respond to its own address at all. Set the chip's Chip number to match the controller's Target chip and the BIST starts passing immediately - there's nothing else wrong with this link yet."/>
</LABCHAIN>
